INT. HOSPITAL BASEMENT LAUNDRY - NIGHT

Industrial washers thump. Steam beads on pipes. A row of orange plastic chairs, bolted down.

EUGENIA HARTSHORN, 60s, in a good coat, folds a single hospital gown over and over, smaller each time. Across from her, her daughter CALLIE HARTSHORN, 30s, scrubs, badge flipped backward, watches a dryer turn.

Between them, on the third chair, a paper bag from the cafeteria. Untouched.

Heavy boots. OSWIN BRADDOCK, 60s, maintenance, enters pushing a cart of towels. He nods to both, parks it, starts feeding sheets into a mangle.

EUGENIA
You haven't eaten.

CALLIE
Neither have you.

EUGENIA
I'm not on shift.

CALLIE
You're here, Mom.

Eugenia smooths the gown flat on her knee. Folds it again.

OSWIN
Evening, ladies.

EUGENIA
Evening.

CALLIE
Hey, Oswin.

He glances at the bag. Then at the two of them. Says nothing. Feeds another sheet.

CALLIE (CONT'D)
They moved him to the sixth floor.

EUGENIA
I know.

CALLIE
I told them to wait.

EUGENIA
I know.

CALLIE
Dr. Rahimi said Thursday. Maybe Friday.

Eugenia sets the gown down. Stands. Walks to the washer. Opens the door. Looks inside. Closes it.

EUGENIA
This one's done.

CALLIE
Mom, it's empty.

EUGENIA
Is it?

Oswin stops the mangle. Eugenia opens the washer again. She reaches in, pulls out a single wool sock, grey, wet. She holds it up.

EUGENIA (CONT'D)
His.

She sits. Holds the sock in both hands, as if it were a bird.

Callie moves to the chair beside her. Does not touch her. Slides the paper bag across. Eugenia takes the sandwich. Unwraps it. Bites. Chews. Her jaw works hard.

Oswin turns off the mangle. Quietly, he fetches a clean towel, folds it, and lays it across the sock in her lap.

OSWIN
Dryer's free.

Eugenia nods. Does not stand.
